<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-superposition" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>A Plane of Cats: Linear Combinations of Images</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The definition of a vector space says nothing about columns of numbers, so anything
      satisfying the axioms of <xref ref="def-vector-space" text="type-global" /> may be
      added and scaled exactly as vectors in <m>\R^n</m> are. We have already seen that
      matrices form such a space. In this section we take the two pictures below
      <mdash /> a cat alive in its box, and the same cat dead in the same box <mdash />
      read each one as a single vector, and form linear combinations of them.<fn>
        The two illustrations show <term>Schrödinger's cat</term>, a famous thought
        experiment created by physicist Erwin Schrödinger in 1935. The physics is deferred
        to <xref ref="subsec-superposition-quantum" text="title" /> at the end of the
        section; until then the pictures are simply two matrices.
      </fn>
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing here is new mathematics. Every step is scalar multiplication, addition,
      span, basis, and coordinates, applied in a vector space whose vectors happen to be
      pictures. That is the point of generalizing: the machinery built for arrows in
      the plane runs unchanged on a space of dimension four hundred thousand.
    </p>
    <p>
      Interactive <term>Sage cells</term> appear throughout. Press <term>Evaluate</term>
      to run one in your browser, or copy the code into
      <url href="https://sagecell.sagemath.org" visual="sagecell.sagemath.org">the Sage
      cell server</url>. Each cell is safe to edit and re-run as often as you like; the
      lines marked <c># change me</c> are the ones worth experimenting with. The cells on
      this page share their variables and must be evaluated in order, so run each one as
      you reach it.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-pictures">
    <title>A Picture Is a Vector</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m> denote the set of all <m>m \times n</m> matrices with
      real entries. The verification in
      <xref ref="eg-m22-vector-space" text="type-global" /> used nothing about the size
      <m>2 \times 2</m>, so it applies word for word to any fixed <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>:
      <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m> is a vector space. Its dimension is <m>mn</m>, since the
      matrices <m>E_{ij}</m> with a <m>1</m> in position <m>(i,j)</m> and zeros elsewhere
      span <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m> and are linearly independent: a linear combination
      <m>\sum_{i,j} c_{ij} E_{ij}</m> is the matrix whose <m>(i,j)</m> entry is
      <m>c_{ij}</m>, and that matrix is zero exactly when every <m>c_{ij}</m> is zero.
    </p>

    <p>
      A grayscale picture is an element of this space. As in
      <xref ref="sec-images-as-matrices" text="title" />, a picture <m>m</m> pixels tall
      and <m>n</m> pixels wide is the <m>m \times n</m> matrix whose <m>(i,j)</m> entry is
      the brightness of the pixel in row <m>i</m> and column <m>j</m>, on a scale where
      <m>0</m> is black and <m>255</m> is white. The two pictures in
      <xref ref="fig-cat-basis" text="global" /> are <m>780</m> pixels tall and
      <m>520</m> pixels wide, so each is a vector in <m>\mathbb{M}_{780,520}</m>, a space
      of dimension <m>405{,}600</m>. Call them
      <md>
        A = \text{the alive cat}, \qquad D = \text{the dead cat}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cat-basis">
      <caption>
        Two vectors in <m>\mathbb{M}_{780,520}</m>. The two pictures share their box,
        their label, and their lighting, so the matrices <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> differ
        mainly where the cat is.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="44% 44%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-alive">
          <caption>The alive cat: the matrix <m>A</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/cat-alive.png">
            <shortdescription>A grayscale illustration of a cat sitting upright inside a wooden box labelled Schrodinger's Cat, beside a mechanism connected to a container marked with a skull and crossbones</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-dead">
          <caption>The dead cat: the matrix <m>D</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/cat-dead.png">
            <shortdescription>The same box and mechanism in grayscale, with the cat lying motionless on the floor of the box and the container gone</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      These two vectors are linearly independent. If one were a scalar multiple of the
      other, say <m>A = cD</m>, then the same <m>c</m> would have to work at every pixel.
      The pixel in row <m>337</m> and column <m>256</m>, in the cat's left eye, has
      <m>A</m> entry <m>210</m> and <m>D</m> entry <m>75</m>, forcing
      <m>c = \frac{210}{75} = \frac{14}{5}</m>; the pixel in row <m>601</m> and column
      <m>301</m>, on the floor of the box, has <m>A</m> entry <m>68</m> and the same
      <m>D</m> entry <m>75</m>, forcing <m>c = \frac{68}{75}</m>. No single scalar does
      both, and reversing the roles of <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> fails for the same reason.
      So <m>\{A, D\}</m> is a linearly independent set of two vectors in a space of
      dimension <m>405{,}600</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-blend">
    <title>Blending the Two Images</title>

    <p>
      The simplest linear combinations of <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> are the ones a screen can
      show. For a <term>blend parameter</term> <m>\lambda</m> between <m>0</m> and
      <m>1</m>, define
      <md>
        S_{\lambda} = (1 - \lambda) A + \lambda D, \qquad 0 \leq \lambda \leq 1.
      </md>
      The coefficient of <m>A</m> is always <m>1 - \lambda</m> and the coefficient of
      <m>D</m> is always <m>\lambda</m>. These two numbers are nonnegative and add to
      <m>1</m>, so each entry of <m>S_\lambda</m> is a weighted average of the
      corresponding entries of <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> and therefore lies between them. Both
      of those lie between <m>0</m> and <m>255</m>, so every blend can be displayed
      exactly as it is computed. No rescaling and no normalization are needed anywhere in
      this subsection.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-cat-blends">
      <title>The five blends drawn in <xref ref="fig-cat-blend" text="global" />.</title>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><m>\lambda</m></cell>
          <cell>displayed matrix <m>S_{\lambda}</m></cell>
          <cell>interpretation</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>0</m></cell>
          <cell><m>A</m></cell>
          <cell>the alive cat</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\frac{1}{4}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{3}{4} A + \frac{1}{4} D</m></cell>
          <cell>mostly <m>A</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{1}{2} A + \frac{1}{2} D</m></cell>
          <cell>an equal blend</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\frac{3}{4}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{1}{4} A + \frac{3}{4} D</m></cell>
          <cell>mostly <m>D</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          <cell><m>D</m></cell>
          <cell>the dead cat</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cat-blend">
      <caption>
        The blend <m>S_{\lambda} = (1 - \lambda) A + \lambda D</m> at
        <m>\lambda = 0, \frac{1}{4}, \frac{1}{2}, \frac{3}{4}, 1</m>. Only the two ends of
        the row are pictures of a cat; everything between them is a vector in
        <m>\mathbb{M}_{780,520}</m> that happens to be drawable.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="18% 18% 18% 18% 18%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-blend-000">
          <caption><m>\lambda = 0</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-blend-000.png">
            <shortdescription>The alive cat alone</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-blend-025">
          <caption><m>\lambda = \frac{1}{4}</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-blend-025.png">
            <shortdescription>The alive cat clearly visible with a faint ghost of the lying cat beneath it</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-blend-050">
          <caption><m>\lambda = \frac{1}{2}</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-blend-050.png">
            <shortdescription>Both cats equally faint, the sitting cat and the lying cat overlapping in the same box</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-blend-075">
          <caption><m>\lambda = \frac{3}{4}</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-blend-075.png">
            <shortdescription>The lying cat clearly visible with a faint ghost of the sitting cat above it</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-blend-100">
          <caption><m>\lambda = 1</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-blend-100.png">
            <shortdescription>The dead cat alone</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      One parameter with one meaning runs through the whole row: <m>\lambda</m> is how far
      the blend has travelled from <m>A</m> towards <m>D</m>. The number that appears in
      the formula, in the caption, and in the pixel arithmetic of the next subsection is
      the same number every time.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-numbers">
    <title>The Arithmetic Behind a Blend</title>

    <p>
      The pictures are made of arithmetic, and the arithmetic is small enough to inspect.
      Take from each picture the patch around the alive cat's left eye <mdash /> the
      same corner of the picture magnified in
      <xref ref="sec-images-as-matrices" text="title" /> <mdash /> and average it in
      blocks down to eight rows and six columns. From the alive cat this gives
      <m>A_0</m>, and from the dead cat, whose box is empty in that corner, it gives
      <m>D_0</m>:
      <md>
        A_0 = \begin{bmatrix}
             65 \amp  63 \amp  80 \amp  84 \amp  81 \amp  79 \\
             73 \amp  86 \amp  90 \amp  88 \amp  90 \amp  87 \\
             81 \amp  89 \amp  86 \amp  90 \amp  93 \amp  86 \\
             89 \amp  68 \amp  76 \amp  69 \amp  95 \amp  91 \\
            103 \amp  91 \amp  78 \amp  87 \amp  91 \amp  91 \\
            100 \amp  99 \amp 108 \amp  83 \amp  99 \amp  94 \\
             90 \amp  97 \amp 104 \amp 100 \amp 102 \amp  86 \\
             84 \amp  94 \amp 110 \amp 107 \amp  70 \amp  63
        \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        D_0 = \begin{bmatrix}
            71 \amp 69 \amp 69 \amp 65 \amp 64 \amp 62 \\
            74 \amp 72 \amp 71 \amp 67 \amp 66 \amp 65 \\
            77 \amp 74 \amp 73 \amp 70 \amp 68 \amp 66 \\
            78 \amp 76 \amp 73 \amp 71 \amp 69 \amp 68 \\
            82 \amp 78 \amp 76 \amp 73 \amp 70 \amp 69 \\
            82 \amp 80 \amp 77 \amp 75 \amp 72 \amp 71 \\
            85 \amp 82 \amp 79 \amp 76 \amp 74 \amp 72 \\
            86 \amp 82 \amp 79 \amp 77 \amp 75 \amp 72
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
      The eye leaves <m>A_0</m> mottled: the pupil darkens the fourth row to <m>68</m>
      and <m>69</m>, and the bright iris below it pushes several entries past
      <m>100</m>. The empty
      wall leaves <m>D_0</m> a smooth gradient, every row a little lighter than the one
      above it and every column a little darker than the one to its left.
    </p>

    <p>
      At <m>\lambda = \frac{1}{2}</m> both coefficients are <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, so the
      blend is the average
      <md>
        \frac{1}{2} A_0 + \frac{1}{2} D_0 = \begin{bmatrix}
            68 \amp 66 \amp 74 \amp 74 \amp 72 \amp 70 \\
            74 \amp 79 \amp 80 \amp 78 \amp 78 \amp 76 \\
            79 \amp 82 \amp 80 \amp 80 \amp 80 \amp 76 \\
            84 \amp 72 \amp 74 \amp 70 \amp 82 \amp 80 \\
            92 \amp 84 \amp 77 \amp 80 \amp 80 \amp 80 \\
            91 \amp 90 \amp 92 \amp 79 \amp 86 \amp 82 \\
            88 \amp 90 \amp 92 \amp 88 \amp 88 \amp 79 \\
            85 \amp 88 \amp 94 \amp 92 \amp 72 \amp 68
        \end{bmatrix},
      </md>
      rounded to integers. Every entry sits halfway between the two originals, which is
      why the eye in <xref ref="fig-cat-blocks" text="global" /> survives the blend at
      half strength. In <m>A_0</m> the pupil entry <m>68</m> lies <m>21</m> below the
      <m>89</m> directly above it; in the average, <m>72</m> lies <m>10</m> below
      <m>82</m>. The contrast, like everything else, has been halved.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cat-blocks">
      <caption>
        The two blocks and their average, magnified so that the individual entries are
        visible. Each square is one entry, on the same scale as the pictures: <m>0</m>
        would be black and <m>255</m> would be white.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="27% 27% 27%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-block-alive">
          <caption><m>A_0</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-block-alive.png">
            <shortdescription>A coarse grid of gray squares with a darker patch where the pupil is</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-block-mix">
          <caption><m>\frac{1}{2} A_0 + \frac{1}{2} D_0</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-block-mix.png">
            <shortdescription>The same grid with the dark patch half as pronounced</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-block-dead">
          <caption><m>D_0</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-block-dead.png">
            <shortdescription>A coarse grid of gray squares shading smoothly from dark at the top to lighter at the bottom</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Enter the two blocks in Sage. The helper <c>show_pictures</c> defined here draws
      each matrix the way <xref ref="fig-cat-blocks" text="global" /> is drawn, with
      <c>vmin=0, vmax=255</c> pinning the ends of the scale so that a given number always
      produces the same shade.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        A0 = matrix(ZZ, [
        [ 65,  63,  80,  84,  81,  79],
        [ 73,  86,  90,  88,  90,  87],
        [ 81,  89,  86,  90,  93,  86],
        [ 89,  68,  76,  69,  95,  91],
        [103,  91,  78,  87,  91,  91],
        [100,  99, 108,  83,  99,  94],
        [ 90,  97, 104, 100, 102,  86],
        [ 84,  94, 110, 107,  70,  63]])
        D0 = matrix(ZZ, [
        [71, 69, 69, 65, 64, 62],
        [74, 72, 71, 67, 66, 65],
        [77, 74, 73, 70, 68, 66],
        [78, 76, 73, 71, 69, 68],
        [82, 78, 76, 73, 70, 69],
        [82, 80, 77, 75, 72, 71],
        [85, 82, 79, 76, 74, 72],
        [86, 82, 79, 77, 75, 72]])

        def show_pictures(*pictures):
            plots = [matrix_plot(M.change_ring(RDF), cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=255)
                     for M in pictures]
            graphics_array(plots, nrows=1).show(figsize=[3*len(plots), 3])

        show_pictures(A0, (1/2)*A0 + (1/2)*D0, D0)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Now put the blend parameter under your own control. The cell below draws
      <m>S_{\lambda}</m> for whatever <m>\lambda</m> you choose with the slider, prints the
      two coefficients, and reports the largest and smallest entry of the result. The
      variable is called <c>lam</c> because <c>lambda</c> is a reserved word in Python.
      Drag from one end to the other and watch one cat fade into the other.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        @interact
        def blend(lam=slider(0, 1, 0.05, default=0.5, label="lambda")):
            S = (1 - lam)*A0 + lam*D0
            print("coefficient of A0 =", n(1 - lam, digits=3),
                  "   coefficient of D0 =", n(lam, digits=3),
                  "   sum =", n((1 - lam) + lam, digits=3))
            print("entries run from", n(min(S.list()), digits=4),
                  "to", n(max(S.list()), digits=4))
            show_pictures(A0, S, D0)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      One experiment is worth running in the cell above: change the slider range from
      <c>slider(0, 1, 0.05, ...)</c> to <c>slider(-1, 2, 0.05, ...)</c> and drag past the
      ends. The two coefficients still add to <m>1</m>, but one of them is now negative,
      so one picture is being subtracted from the other and the printed range leaves
      <m>0</m> to <m>255</m>. The drawing does not fail gracefully: every
      entry below <m>0</m> is drawn as black and every entry above <m>255</m> as white, so
      whole regions collapse to a single shade. The vector <m>(1 - \lambda)A + \lambda D</m>
      exists for every real <m>\lambda</m>. It is only the drawing that fails.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-span">
    <title>From Blends to Span</title>

    <p>
      That experiment separates three sets of pictures, each larger than the last. The
      blends themselves are the line segment
      <md>
        L = \left\{ (1 - \lambda) A + \lambda D \; \middle| \; 0 \leq \lambda \leq 1
        \right\},
      </md>
      whose endpoints are <m>A</m> and <m>D</m>. Letting <m>\lambda</m> be any real number
      gives the whole affine line through <m>A</m> and <m>D</m>, the combinations
      <m>xA + yD</m> whose coefficients still satisfy <m>x + y = 1</m>. Dropping that
      condition too, and letting the two coefficients vary independently, gives everything:
      by <xref ref="thm-span-is-subspace" text="type-global" />,
      <md>
        W = \operatorname{Span}\{A, D\}
        = \left\{ xA + yD \; \middle| \; x, y \in \R \right\}
      </md>
      is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{M}_{780,520}</m>, and since <m>\{A, D\}</m> is linearly
      independent it is a basis for <m>W</m>. So <m>W</m> is a two-dimensional subspace of
      a space of dimension <m>405{,}600</m>: a plane of cats, sitting inside an enormous
      space of pictures. The five pictures we displayed occupy a segment of one line in
      that plane.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two dimensions means two coordinates. With <m>\mathcal{B} = \{A, D\}</m>, the
      coordinate vector of <m>X = xA + yD</m> in the sense of
      <xref ref="def-coordinate-vector" text="type-global" /> is
      <md>
        [X]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} \in \R^2,
      </md>
      and by <xref ref="thm-coordinates-unique" text="type-global" /> no other pair of
      scalars produces the same picture. The coordinate mapping is an isomorphism from
      <m>W</m> to <m>\R^2</m>, so by <xref ref="rem-isomorphic-same" text="type-global" />
      the plane of cats <em>is</em> <m>\R^2</m> for every purpose that involves adding and
      scaling. In those coordinates the blends are the segment joining
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> to
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the affine line is the line through
      those two points, and <m>W</m> is the whole plane.
    </p>

    <p>
      The cell below reports, for coefficients of your choosing, the range of entries in
      <m>xA_0 + yD_0</m> and whether a screen could show it. Only the first of the three
      combinations it runs is a blend; the second is a legitimate vector of <m>W</m> that
      exaggerates the difference between the two pictures instead of interpolating between
      them, and the third leaves the displayable range entirely. Watch what a screen does with each.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        def report(x, y):
            X = x*A0 + y*D0
            lo, hi = min(X.list()), max(X.list())
            print("x =", x, "  y =", y,
                  "  entries from", n(lo, digits=4), "to", n(hi, digits=4),
                  "  displayable?", bool(0 &lt;= lo and hi &lt;= 255))
            show_pictures(X)

        report(3/4, 1/4)   # change me: a blend, since x, y are nonnegative and x + y = 1
        report(2, -1)      # on the affine line, but outside the segment of blends
        report(2, 2)       # in W, and too bright for a screen: every entry is doubled
      </input>
    </sage>

    <question xml:id="rem-superposition-not-subspace">
      <title>Do Displayable Pictures Form a Subspace?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Only some of the vectors in <m>W</m> can be put on a screen, and it is worth
          saying what that means in the language of this chapter. Consider the set
          <md>
            \mathcal{P} = \left\{ M \in \mathbb{M}_{mn} \; \middle| \;
              0 \leq m_{ij} \leq 255 \text{ for all } i, j \right\}
          </md>
          of pictures a screen can show. Is <m>\mathcal{P}</m> a subspace of
          <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          No. <m>\mathcal{P}</m> contains <m>A</m> but not <m>2A</m>, so it fails closure
          under scalar multiplication, and it contains <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> but not
          <m>A + D</m>: the brightest entries of the two pictures are <m>214</m> and
          <m>184</m>, and their sum <m>398</m> is far outside the range. It is a bounded
          box inside the vector space, not a subspace of it. The vector space is the
          larger, simpler object: the arithmetic is unrestricted, and only the last step
          <mdash /> looking at the answer <mdash /> requires the entries to be in range.
          Blends are exactly the combinations for which that last step is free.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </question>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-basis">
    <title>Average and Difference</title>

    <p>
      A plane has more than one basis, and a second one is worth having here because its
      vectors mean something about images. Set
      <md>
        M = \frac{1}{2}\left( A + D \right), \qquad
        \Delta = \frac{1}{2}\left( A - D \right).
      </md>
      The <term>average</term> <m>M</m> is the middle picture of
      <xref ref="fig-cat-blend" text="global" />, the blend at <m>\lambda = \frac{1}{2}</m>.
      The <term>difference</term> <m>\Delta</m> records where the two pictures disagree:
      its entries are close to zero on the box, the label, and the lighting, which the two
      pictures very nearly share, and they are largest where a cat is. Its entries run from
      <m>-52</m> to <m>76</m>, so it is not itself displayable: it is negative wherever
      the dead picture is the brighter of the two. For that reason
      <xref ref="fig-cat-difference" text="global" /> shows <m>128J + \Delta</m>, where
      <m>J</m> is the matrix of all ones; mid-gray then marks the pixels where the two
      pictures agree.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cat-difference">
      <caption>
        The difference <m>\Delta = \frac{1}{2}(A - D)</m>, shifted by <m>128J</m> so that
        it can be displayed: mid-gray marks the pixels where the two pictures agree, and
        the departures from mid-gray are exactly where they do not. The box and the label
        nearly cancel, leaving a faint outline; the two cats do not cancel at all.
      </caption>
      <image source="images/cat-difference.png" width="44%">
        <shortdescription>An almost uniformly gray picture in which the box and its label survive only as a faint outline, while two ghostly cats remain visible, one sitting and one lying</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Adding and subtracting the two definitions recovers the original pictures,
      <md>
        A = M + \Delta, \qquad D = M - \Delta,
      </md>
      so each of <m>\{A, D\}</m> and <m>\{M, \Delta\}</m> lies in the span of the other.
      Hence <m>\mathcal{C} = \{M, \Delta\}</m> is a second basis for the same plane
      <m>W</m>. Substituting these expressions into a blend gives
      <md>
        S_{\lambda} = (1 - \lambda)(M + \Delta) + \lambda(M - \Delta)
        = M + (1 - 2\lambda) \Delta.
      </md>
      Read in this basis, the row of pictures in <xref ref="fig-cat-blend" text="global" />
      does one thing only: the average component stays fixed at <m>M</m> while the
      difference coefficient <m>1 - 2\lambda</m> slides from <m>+1</m> down to <m>-1</m>.
      At <m>\lambda = \frac{1}{2}</m> the difference coefficient is <m>0</m>, and the
      blend is exactly the average.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same substitution works for any vector of <m>W</m>, not just a blend. For
      <m>X = xA + yD</m>,
      <md>
        X = x(M + \Delta) + y(M - \Delta) = (x + y) M + (x - y) \Delta,
      </md>
      which is the change of basis of
      <xref ref="def-change-of-basis" text="type-global" /> made explicit:
      <md>
        [X]_{\mathcal{C}} = P [X]_{\mathcal{B}}, \qquad
        P = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
      This <m>P</m> satisfies <m>P^2 = 2 I_2</m>, so the return trip is
      <m>P^{-1} = \frac{1}{2} P</m>: halving the sum and difference of the
      <m>\mathcal{C}</m>-coordinates recovers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
      <xref ref="tab-cat-coordinates" text="global" /> puts the two descriptions of the
      same picture side by side.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-cat-coordinates">
      <title>One vector <m>X</m>, two bases.</title>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>basis</cell>
          <cell>expansion of <m>X</m></cell>
          <cell>coordinate vector</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\mathcal{B} = \{A, D\}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>X = xA + yD</m></cell>
          <cell><m>[X]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\mathcal{C} = \{M, \Delta\}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>X = (x + y) M + (x - y) \Delta</m></cell>
          <cell><m>[X]_{\mathcal{C}} = \begin{bmatrix} x + y \\ x - y \end{bmatrix}</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The picture <m>X</m> has not changed; only its coordinates have, because the basis
      has. The alive cat is a basis vector of <m>\mathcal{B}</m> and has coordinates
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> there, while in <m>\mathcal{C}</m> its
      coordinates are <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>: a picture that is one
      basis vector in one basis is an even combination of both in the other. Being a
      combination of two things is not a property of the vector. It is a property of the
      vector <em>together with the basis</em> you chose to describe it, exactly as a
      vector in <m>\R^2</m> has no coordinates until a basis is named.
    </p>

    <p>
      The cell below builds <m>M</m> and <m>\Delta</m> out of the blocks, checks that
      <m>P</m> really does convert coordinates, and draws both. The second picture needs
      the shift used in <xref ref="fig-cat-difference" text="global" />.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        M0     = (A0 + D0).change_ring(RDF)/2
        Delta0 = (A0 - D0).change_ring(RDF)/2
        P = matrix(RDF, [[1, 1], [1, -1]])

        x, y = RDF(0.75), RDF(0.25)     # change me: any two coefficients at all
        X = x*A0 + y*D0
        coordsB = vector(RDF, [x, y])
        coordsC = P*coordsB
        print("coordinates in B:", coordsB)
        print("coordinates in C:", coordsC)
        print("same picture?",
              (coordsC[0]*M0 + coordsC[1]*Delta0 - X).norm() &lt; 1e-9)
        print("P^2 = 2I?",
              (P*P - 2*identity_matrix(RDF, 2)).norm() &lt; 1e-9)
        print("back again?",
              ((P/2)*coordsC - coordsB).norm() &lt; 1e-9)

        J = ones_matrix(RDF, 8, 6)
        show_pictures(M0, 128*J + Delta0)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      The checks compare computed quantities with the ones they should equal by measuring
      how far apart they are, rather than asking whether they are equal on the nose. Floating
      point arithmetic rounds at every step, so <c>==</c> can report <c>False</c> for two
      quantities that differ only in the sixteenth digit; the mathematics is exact even
      when the arithmetic is not. Change <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> and evaluate again: the
      coordinates change in both bases, and every check still passes.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-quantum">
    <title>An Optional Connection to Quantum Mechanics</title>

    <p>
      Everything above is finished linear algebra, and none of what follows is needed for
      any of it. But the pictures were chosen for a reason, and the word for a linear
      combination of states in physics is worth meeting once.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-superposition-quantum">
      <title>For the Interested Reader</title>
      <p>
        Quantum mechanics describes a system that can be found in either of two states by
        a linear combination of those states, called a <term>superposition</term>. Writing
        the two states as our two matrices, it would take the form
        <m>\alpha A + \beta D</m>. The resemblance to <m>S_{\lambda}</m> is real but
        limited. There the coefficients are <term>amplitudes</term>, generally complex
        numbers subject to <m>|\alpha|^2 + |\beta|^2 = 1</m>, and when the two states form
        an orthonormal basis their squared magnitudes <m>|\alpha|^2</m> and
        <m>|\beta|^2</m> are the probabilities of the two outcomes of a measurement. The
        blends in this section are classical linear combinations of two images, not
        representations of quantum states.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark xml:id="rem-superposition-disclaimer">
      <title>This Is Not What a Superposition Really Is</title>
      <p>
        Any picture of a superposition is a depiction, and the depiction will not be
        accurate: superposition has no faithful classical picture. The blends above are
        worth drawing because they show linear combinations, not because they show physics.
        We refer the interested reader to the following lecture by Richard Feynman on
        quantum mechanical behavior.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-feynman-lecture">
        <caption>Richard Feynman lecturing on quantum mechanical behavior.</caption>
        <video xml:id="video-feynman-lecture" youtube="xNF_3KdpdrY" width="70%" />
      </figure>
    </remark>

    <p>
      Three differences are worth stating plainly.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>The real state space is complex.</term> Quantum states are vectors in a
            complex vector space, so the amplitudes are complex numbers. All ten axioms of
            <xref ref="def-vector-space" text="type-global" /> hold with
            <m>\mathbb{C}</m> in place of <m>\R</m>, and everything in this chapter
            carries over unchanged. Keeping the coefficients real, as we did, keeps the
            pictures drawable; what it leaves out is the phase a complex amplitude
            carries, and the interference that comes with it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>A superposition is not a blurred picture.</term> Opening the box
            produces one cat or the other, never the blend in the middle of
            <xref ref="fig-cat-blend" text="global" />. A faded picture depicts the
            coefficients, not what anyone would see.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Adding pictures is not adding states.</term> Averaging two pictures is
            a statement about brightness, and nothing physical happens when it is done.
            The combination means what it means here only because we declared these two
            pictures to stand for the two states; what you are looking at is a picture of
            <m>1 - \lambda</m> and <m>\lambda</m>, not of light leaving a box.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      What survives the caveats is the part this chapter is about. Two objects that are
      not columns of numbers <mdash /> two photographs, or two quantum states <mdash />
      can be added and scaled, and once that is granted, span, linear independence, basis,
      dimension, coordinates, and change of basis all apply without a word of
      modification. That is what the axioms of
      <xref ref="def-vector-space" text="type-global" /> bought us.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
